{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The proposed relation is stated as 'contradicts', but the content merely opposes SRC_002's view on one claim (framing discrepancy-driven discovery as checklist insufficiency vs. reliability gain). That is a disagreement on interpretation, not a logical contradiction. More critically, the 'refuted if' clause — identification of unlisted variables 'consistently leads to the discovery of critical failure modes the expert also missed' — is a tautological escape hatch: it makes the claim unfalsifiable by requiring consistent discovery of critical modes, a condition that can never be reliably checked (we cannot know what experts missed until after the fact, and 'consistently' sets a bar no finite trial can satisfy). This turns the relation into an assertion shielded by an impossible counterfactual rather than a substantive, testable relation between the endpoints.","target":"6380e59bb5cfc93ecc1e7ea73dccb58731f5bfd62dd3b0ba62c23e6ac10cd30d"}]}